<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gran Sasso</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gran Sasso d'Italia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Gran</a> Sasso d'Italia is the highest massif of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzo</a>, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, rising to 2,912 metres at Corno Grande. In antiquity its valleys were home to the Vestini. In September 1943 German paratroopers freed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> from the Campo Imperatore hotel on the massif, after which he headed the Italian Social Republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gran-sasso.jpg" alt="Gran Sasso" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fiat 500e · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Corno_grande_da_campo_imperatore.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
